\description{
Start a \href{https://modelcontextprotocol.io}{Model Context Protocol} (MCP)
server available through HTTP (Streamable HTTP transport) to give remote
MCP clients (e.g., Claude Code) access to the MDBs of a chTKCat instance.
The server provides the same read-only tools as \code{\link[=chTKCat_mcp_server]{chTKCat_mcp_server()}}.
}
\details{
The server is meant to be run as a service behind a reverse proxy
(e.g., nginx) providing HTTPS: by default it only listens on the local
interface (\code{mcpHost = "127.0.0.1"}).
See \code{vignette("TKCat-MCP", package = "TKCat")} for deployment instructions.

\strong{Authentication.} Clients authenticate with their chTKCat (ClickHouse)
credentials provided in an HTTP Basic \code{Authorization} header
(\verb{Authorization: Basic <base64 of "user:password">}). Credentials are
checked by ClickHouse and each client gets access to the MDBs readable by
its own user. No credential is stored by the server, except in memory
to keep the connections open (during \code{cacheTime} seconds).
If \code{anonymousUser} is set (e.g., "default"), requests without
\code{Authorization} header are processed with this user without password.
\strong{Credentials are sent in clear text if HTTPS is not used.}

\strong{Read-only access.} As for \code{\link[=chTKCat_mcp_server]{chTKCat_mcp_server()}}, ClickHouse sessions
are opened with the \code{readonly = 2} setting and SQL queries are checked
before being sent.

\strong{Protocol.} The server is stateless: it does not create MCP sessions and
answers each POST request with a single JSON response. It does not provide
server-sent event streams (GET requests are rejected with a 405 status).
Several instances can thus be run behind a load balancer.

\strong{Logging.} Tool calls are logged on the standard error output
(time, client address, user and tool name).
